PG.notes("l160", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces the boss-level project: a browser version of the Simon memory game built with jQuery plus earlier-module skills.</p>\n<h3>How the game behaves</h3>\n<ul><li>Press any key to start; the game flashes one coloured tile and the player must click it.</li><li>Each level adds one more tile to the sequence, so the player has to remember and repeat a longer pattern every round.</li><li>A wrong click ends the game with a failure message, and pressing any key restarts from level one.</li><li>The challenge tests jQuery from the previous section plus JavaScript from earlier modules (arrays, functions, conditionals, timing).</li></ul>\n<h3>What comes next</h3>\n<ul><li>The following lessons supply instructions and starter files, then walk through the challenge in numbered steps, each with an answer lecture.</li></ul>\n<h2>The task: Simon Game (overview)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter files are provided in the next lessons (index.html, styles.css, sounds folder).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Build a working Simon game: keypress to start, growing random sequence shown with animation and sound, user clicks checked against it, game over and restart.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Download the starter files (Step 0).</li><li>Work through Steps 1-9 in order, checking each answer lecture afterwards.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A playable Simon game that increases in level each round and resets on a wrong click.</p>\n</div>");
PG.notes("l161", "<p class=\"summary\">Link to the live Simon game so the learner can try it before building it.</p>\n<h3>What to do</h3>\n<ul><li>Open the hosted demo of the Simon game (londonappbrewery.github.io/Simon-Game) and play a few rounds first.</li><li>Watch the pattern: the game flashes one colour with a sound, you repeat it, then it adds one more colour each level; a wrong click ends the game.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Note the details you will need to reproduce: title text changes per level, buttons flash/animate when pressed, red flash on game over, any key restarts.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l162", "<p class=\"summary\">Download and open the Simon starter project and look around before coding.</p>\n<h3>Starter project</h3>\n<ul><li>Download the zip from the Course Resources button (top left of the lesson), unzip it and open the folder in the code editor.</li><li>Contents: index.html with four pre-styled button divs, styles.css, and a sounds folder with one mp3 per colour plus a wrong-answer sound.</li><li>Open index.html in Chrome (copy its full path into the address bar) to see the static layout.</li><li>The following lessons are numbered steps that add the behaviour one piece at a time.</li></ul>\n<h3>Pieces the HTML already provides</h3>\n<ul><li>An h1 with id level-title, initially 'Press A Key to Start'.</li><li>Four divs with class btn plus a colour class, each with an id equal to the colour (green, red, yellow, blue). Those ids are what the JS will use to pick buttons and name sound files.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> styles.css already defines .pressed (grey + white glow) and .game-over (red body, 0.8 opacity), which later steps toggle.</li></ul>\n<h2>The task: Open the Simon starter</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Zip from Course Resources</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Have the starter running in Chrome</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Download zip</li><li>Unzip and open in editor</li><li>Inspect index.html, styles.css and sounds folder</li><li>Open index.html in Chrome</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Static page with a title and four coloured, non-functional buttons.</p>\n</div>");
PG.notes("l163", "<p class=\"summary\">Create game.js, link it, then add jQuery and verify both loaded.</p>\n<h3>Why an external file</h3>\n<ul><li>All game logic lives in a separate JS file linked from the HTML, keeping markup and behaviour apart.</li></ul>\n<h3>Verifying the setup</h3>\n<ul><li>A temporary alert() in game.js proves the file is linked: reload the page and the dialog should appear. Remove or comment it out afterwards.</li><li>Add the jQuery library (CDN script tag) before the game script so $ exists when the game file runs.</li><li>Test jQuery in DevTools: typing $(&quot;h1&quot;) in the console should print a jQuery object wrapping the h1; if jQuery did not load you get a '$ is not defined' error.</li></ul>\n<h3>Script tag placement</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Put both script tags at the end of body so elements exist when the code runs, and jQuery's tag must come first.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Script tags at end of body</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;script src=&quot;https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.7.1/jquery.min.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;script src=&quot;game.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Link JS and jQuery</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter project</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> game.js linked and jQuery loaded</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create game.js</li><li>Add a script tag for it in index.html</li><li>Add alert(&quot;hello&quot;) and confirm it fires, then delete it</li><li>Add the jQuery CDN script tag above game.js</li><li>In the console run $(&quot;h1&quot;) and confirm a jQuery object is returned</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Page loads with no console errors and $(&quot;h1&quot;) works.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>jQuery script placed after game.js causes '$ is not defined'.</li><li>The reference repo names the file script.js; the lecture uses game.js. Either works if the link matches.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l164", "<p class=\"summary\">Pointer to the downloadable solution for step 1.</p>\n<h3>How to use it</h3>\n<ul><li>Pointer lecture: the full code for steps up to 1 is a downloadable project under Course Resources. The notes below restate the step's solution from the reference repo.</li><li>Solutions are not unique; any code that produces the required behaviour counts.</li><li>The downloaded solution marks each step with comments so you can jump to the relevant lines.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 1 end state</h3>\n<ul><li>index.html ends with the jQuery CDN script followed by the game script, both at the bottom of body. The reference repo links ./script.js.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference index.html bottom</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;script src=&quot;https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.7.1/jquery.min.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;script src=&quot;./script.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Reference repo's HTML has a typo (lass=&quot;row&quot; instead of class) and an author footer; ignore both.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l165", "<p class=\"summary\">Generate a random colour and append it to the game's pattern array.</p>\n<h3>Data structures</h3>\n<ul><li>buttonColours is a global array holding red, blue, green, yellow in a fixed order; index 0-3 maps to a colour.</li><li>gamePattern is a global empty array that grows by one colour per level.</li></ul>\n<h3>Random number</h3>\n<ul><li>Math.random() returns a float in [0,1) and never 1. Multiply by 4 and wrap in Math.floor() to get an integer 0-3.</li><li>Use the console to sanity check the range.</li></ul>\n<h3>Picking and storing</h3>\n<ul><li>randomChosenColour = buttonColours[randomNumber].</li><li>gamePattern.push(randomChosenColour) adds it to the end.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">nextSequence so far</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var buttonColours = [&quot;red&quot;, &quot;blue&quot;, &quot;green&quot;, &quot;yellow&quot;];\nvar gamePattern = [];\n\nfunction nextSequence() {\n  var randomNumber = Math.floor(Math.random() * 4);\n  var randomChosenColour = buttonColours[randomNumber];\n  gamePattern.push(randomChosenColour);\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Create the next pattern entry</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> game.js linked with jQuery</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> nextSequence() appends a random colour to gamePattern</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create function nextSequence()</li><li>Inside, make randomNumber 0-3 (Math.floor(Math.random()*4))</li><li>At the top create buttonColours array in order red, blue, green, yellow</li><li>Make randomChosenColour from the array using randomNumber</li><li>At the top create empty gamePattern</li><li>push randomChosenColour onto gamePattern</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Calling nextSequence() in the console grows gamePattern by one random colour each call.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Math.random()*3 gives only 0-2 after flooring; multiply by 4.</li><li>Declare the arrays outside the function or they reset each call.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l166", "<p class=\"summary\">Solution for step 2: random colour appended to gamePattern.</p>\n<h3>Walkthrough</h3>\n<ul><li>Pointer lecture: the full code for steps up to 2 is a downloadable project under Course Resources. The notes below restate the step's solution from the reference repo.</li><li>Math.floor(Math.random() * 4) yields 0,1,2 or 3; indexes buttonColours; push stores the result in gamePattern.</li><li>The reference final code keeps the same lines inside nextSequence().</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference excerpt</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var randomNumber = Math.floor(Math.random() * 4);\nvar randomChosenColour = buttonColours[randomNumber];\ngamePattern.push(randomChosenColour);</pre>");
PG.notes("l167", "<p class=\"summary\">Flash the chosen button and play its sound, using search to find the techniques.</p>\n<h3>Requirements</h3>\n<ul><li>Select the button whose id matches randomChosenColour with the jQuery id selector: $(&quot;#&quot; + randomChosenColour).</li><li>Animate a flash on it. Searching 'animate flash jQuery' leads to chaining fadeIn/fadeOut.</li><li>Play the matching sound with plain JS; search 'play sound javascript' leads to the Audio object.</li><li>The lecture deliberately asks the learner to research instead of giving the method names.</li></ul>\n<h3>Hints</h3>\n<ul><li>id selectors need the # prefix.</li><li>Sound files are named after the colours (blue.mp3 etc.).</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">One possible approach</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">$(&quot;#&quot; + randomChosenColour).fadeIn(100).fadeOut(100).fadeIn(100);\nvar audio = new Audio(&quot;sounds/&quot; + randomChosenColour + &quot;.mp3&quot;);\naudio.play();</pre>\n<h2>The task: Show the next colour</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> nextSequence() picks a colour</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Button flashes and its sound plays on each call</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Select the button by id with jQuery</li><li>Add a flash animation</li><li>Play its mp3 with JavaScript</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Each nextSequence() call flashes one button with sound.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Audio path must match the sounds folder and filename case.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l168", "<p class=\"summary\">Solution for step 3: flash and sound inside nextSequence.</p>\n<h3>Walkthrough</h3>\n<ul><li>Pointer lecture: the full code for steps up to 3 is a downloadable project under Course Resources. The notes below restate the step's solution from the reference repo.</li><li>fadeIn(100).fadeOut(100).fadeIn(100) chains three 100 ms fades, producing a quick flash.</li><li>new Audio(path).play() creates and plays a sound.</li><li>The reference passes randomNumber to playSound after the refactor in step 5, which would look up 0-3.mp3; see pitfalls.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference excerpt</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">$(&quot;#&quot; + randomChosenColour)\n  .fadeIn(100)\n  .fadeOut(100)\n  .fadeIn(100);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo calls playSound(randomNumber) (a number) when it should be playSound(randomChosenColour); files are named by colour, so no sound would play. Use the colour name.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l169", "<p class=\"summary\">Detect the user's button clicks and record the clicked colour in userClickedPattern.</p>\n<h3>Click handling</h3>\n<ul><li>All four buttons share class btn, so $(&quot;.btn&quot;).click(handler) attaches one handler to all.</li><li>The handler is an anonymous function.</li><li>Inside it, this is the clicked element; $(this).attr(&quot;id&quot;) reads its id, e.g. 'green'.</li></ul>\n<h3>Storing</h3>\n<ul><li>Declare userClickedPattern = [] at the top and push userChosenColour into it.</li><li>Log the array to confirm it builds up as you click.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Handler</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var userClickedPattern = [];\n\n$(&quot;.btn&quot;).click(function () {\n  var userChosenColour = $(this).attr(&quot;id&quot;);\n  userClickedPattern.push(userChosenColour);\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: Record user clicks</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> gamePattern logic done</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> userClickedPattern fills as buttons are clicked</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add click handler on .btn</li><li>Store id in userChosenColour via $(this).attr(&quot;id&quot;)</li><li>Create empty userClickedPattern at top</li><li>Push the colour</li><li>console.log to check</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Console shows e.g. ['red','blue'] after two clicks.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Arrow functions change this in the handler; use function() so $(this) works.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l170", "<p class=\"summary\">Solution for step 4: click handler storing ids.</p>\n<h3>Walkthrough</h3>\n<ul><li>Pointer lecture: the full code for steps up to 4 is a downloadable project under Course Resources. The notes below restate the step's solution from the reference repo.</li><li>The handler reads the id attribute via attr(&quot;id&quot;) and pushes it to userClickedPattern.</li><li>Later steps add playSound, animatePress and checkAnswer calls to the same handler.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference excerpt</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">$(&quot;.btn&quot;).click(function () {\n    var userChosenColour = $(this).attr(&quot;id&quot;);\n    userClickedPattern.push(userChosenColour);\n});</pre>");
PG.notes("l171", "<p class=\"summary\">Refactor sound playing into a reusable playSound(name) function.</p>\n<h3>Refactor</h3>\n<ul><li>Create playSound(name) taking the colour name.</li><li>Move the Audio code from nextSequence into it, building the path as &quot;sounds/&quot; + name + &quot;.mp3&quot;.</li><li>Call playSound(randomChosenColour) in nextSequence and playSound(userChosenColour) in the click handler.</li><li>Same function also serves &quot;wrong&quot; later because wrong.mp3 follows the same naming.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">playSound</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function playSound(name) {\n  var audio = new Audio(&quot;sounds/&quot; + name + &quot;.mp3&quot;);\n  audio.play();\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Extract playSound</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Step 3 sound code in nextSequence</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Both game and user presses play the right sound</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create playSound(name)</li><li>Move the audio code there with name in the path</li><li>Call it from nextSequence</li><li>Call it from the click handler</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Clicking green plays green.mp3; the pattern flash plays its colour sound.</p>\n</div>");
PG.notes("l172", "<p class=\"summary\">Solution for step 5: playSound helper.</p>\n<h3>Walkthrough</h3>\n<ul><li>Pointer lecture: the full code for steps up to 5 is a downloadable project under Course Resources. The notes below restate the step's solution from the reference repo.</li><li>One function removes duplicated audio code; the parameter is the file's base name.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference excerpt</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function playSound(name) {\n    var audio = new Audio(&quot;./sounds/&quot; + name + &quot;.mp3&quot;);\n    audio.play();\n}</pre>");
PG.notes("l173", "<p class=\"summary\">Add a pressed visual effect for 100 ms on clicked buttons.</p>\n<h3>animatePress(currentColour)</h3>\n<ul><li>Takes the colour name and selects $(&quot;#&quot; + currentColour).</li><li>The .pressed class in styles.css adds a white box-shadow and grey background.</li><li>addClass(&quot;pressed&quot;) applies it; setTimeout with 100 ms later calls removeClass(&quot;pressed&quot;).</li><li>Call animatePress(userChosenColour) from the click handler.</li></ul>\n<h3>Delay</h3>\n<ul><li>setTimeout(fn, ms) runs fn once after the delay; search 'how to add delay javascript' finds it.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">animatePress</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function animatePress(currentColour) {\n  $(&quot;#&quot; + currentColour).addClass(&quot;pressed&quot;);\n  setTimeout(function () {\n    $(&quot;#&quot; + currentColour).removeClass(&quot;pressed&quot;);\n  }, 100);\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Press animation</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Click handler exists</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Clicked button briefly looks pressed</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create animatePress(currentColour)</li><li>Add class pressed</li><li>Remove it after 100ms with setTimeout</li><li>Call from handler</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Buttons flash grey with glow on click.</p>\n</div>");
PG.notes("l174", "<p class=\"summary\">Solution for step 6: animatePress.</p>\n<h3>Walkthrough</h3>\n<ul><li>Pointer lecture: the full code for steps up to 6 is a downloadable project under Course Resources. The notes below restate the step's solution from the reference repo.</li><li>addClass then setTimeout(removeClass, 100) creates a momentary state change.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference excerpt</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function animatePress(currentColour) {\n    $(&quot;#&quot; + currentColour).addClass(&quot;pressed&quot;);\n    setTimeout(function () {\n        $(&quot;#&quot; + currentColour).removeClass(&quot;pressed&quot;);\n    }, 100);\n}</pre>");
PG.notes("l175", "<p class=\"summary\">Start on the first key press and track and display the level.</p>\n<h3>State</h3>\n<ul><li>started = false flag so only the first keypress starts the game.</li><li>level = 0 counter.</li></ul>\n<h3>Key detection</h3>\n<ul><li>$(document).keydown(fn) listens for any key.</li><li>If !started: set h1 text to 'Level 0' (via $(&quot;#level-title&quot;).text(...)), call nextSequence(), set started = true.</li></ul>\n<h3>In nextSequence</h3>\n<ul><li>Increment level by 1 each call and update the h1 text to 'Level ' + level.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The lecture's Level 0 text is immediately replaced by Level 1 in the reference, since nextSequence updates it.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Start logic</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var started = false;\nvar level = 0;\n\n$(document).keydown(function () {\n  if (!started) {\n    $(&quot;#level-title&quot;).text(&quot;Level &quot; + level);\n    nextSequence();\n    started = true;\n  }\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: Start on keypress</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Click and pattern code working</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> First key starts game, title shows level</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Detect keydown on document</li><li>Track started flag</li><li>Add level variable</li><li>Set h1 text</li><li>In nextSequence, level++ and update h1</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Title changes from prompt to Level 1 on first key; further keys do nothing.</p>\n</div>");
PG.notes("l176", "<p class=\"summary\">Solution for step 7: started flag, level, keydown.</p>\n<h3>Walkthrough</h3>\n<ul><li>Pointer lecture: the full code for steps up to 7 is a downloadable project under Course Resources. The notes below restate the step's solution from the reference repo.</li><li>started prevents a second start; level++ occurs inside nextSequence().</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference nextSequence excerpt</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">level++;\n$(&quot;#level-title&quot;).text(&quot;Level &quot; + level);</pre>");
PG.notes("l177", "<p class=\"summary\">Compare the user's latest click to the pattern and advance the level when the sequence is complete.</p>\n<h3>Game rules</h3>\n<ul><li>Game shows first colour; user repeats; then game adds another, user repeats whole sequence; one mistake ends the game.</li></ul>\n<h3>checkAnswer(currentLevel)</h3>\n<ul><li>Called from the click handler with userClickedPattern.length - 1, the index of the latest click (4 clicks -&gt; index 3).</li><li>Compare gamePattern[currentLevel] === userClickedPattern[currentLevel]; log 'success' or 'wrong' while debugging.</li><li>If correct and userClickedPattern.length === gamePattern.length, the user finished the sequence: call nextSequence() after setTimeout 1000 ms.</li><li>nextSequence() resets userClickedPattern to [] for the next round.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">checkAnswer</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function checkAnswer(currentLevel) {\n  if (gamePattern[currentLevel] === userClickedPattern[currentLevel]) {\n    if (userClickedPattern.length === gamePattern.length) {\n      setTimeout(function () { nextSequence(); }, 1000);\n    }\n  } else {\n    console.log(&quot;wrong&quot;);\n  }\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Check the answer</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Level and click tracking done</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Game advances when user repeats sequence</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create checkAnswer(currentLevel)</li><li>Call it with userClickedPattern.length - 1</li><li>Compare latest entries</li><li>If right and lengths match, nextSequence after 1000ms</li><li>Reset userClickedPattern in nextSequence</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Game is playable until the first wrong click.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Compare only the last index per click, not whole arrays.</li><li>Forgetting to reset userClickedPattern breaks later levels.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l178", "<p class=\"summary\">Solution for step 8: checkAnswer logic.</p>\n<h3>Walkthrough</h3>\n<ul><li>Pointer lecture: the full code for steps up to 8 is a downloadable project under Course Resources. The notes below restate the step's solution from the reference repo.</li><li>=== compares strings; length equality means round complete; the 1 s delay lets the user see their last click before the next flash.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference excerpt</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">checkAnswer(userClickedPattern.length - 1);\n\nif (userClickedPattern.length === gamePattern.length) {\n    setTimeout(function () {\n        nextSequence();\n    }, 1000);\n}</pre>");
PG.notes("l179", "<p class=\"summary\">Add game-over feedback: sound, flash and title message.</p>\n<h3>On a wrong answer</h3>\n<ul><li>playSound(&quot;wrong&quot;) (wrong.mp3 in sounds).</li><li>Add class game-over to body, remove after 200 ms with setTimeout; the CSS turns the body red with reduced opacity.</li><li>Change the h1 to 'Game Over, Press Any Key to Restart'.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Else branch</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">playSound(&quot;wrong&quot;);\n$(&quot;body&quot;).addClass(&quot;game-over&quot;);\nsetTimeout(function () {\n  $(&quot;body&quot;).removeClass(&quot;game-over&quot;);\n}, 200);\n$(&quot;#level-title&quot;).text(&quot;Game Over, Press Any Key to Restart&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Game over feedback</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> checkAnswer logs 'wrong'</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Wrong click plays sound, flashes red, shows message</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Play wrong sound</li><li>Add and remove game-over on body</li><li>Update h1 text</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Red flash, buzzer, new title.</p>\n</div>");
PG.notes("l180", "<p class=\"summary\">Solution for step 9: game over branch (restart comes in step 10).</p>\n<h3>Walkthrough</h3>\n<ul><li>Pointer lecture: the full code for steps up to 9 is a downloadable project under Course Resources. The notes below restate the step's solution from the reference repo.</li><li>The reference also calls startOver() here, which belongs to step 10: it resets level, gamePattern and started.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference startOver</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function startOver() {\n    level = 0;\n    gamePattern = [];\n    started = false;\n}</pre>");
PG.notes("l181", "<p class=\"summary\">Final step of the Simon build: write a startOver() function that puts the game's state variables back to their starting values, and call it when the player presses a wrong button so a key press can begin a fresh game.</p>\n<h3>Game state that must be reset</h3>\n<ul><li>Three global variables control a run of the game: level (the current round number), gamePattern (the array of colours the computer has chosen so far) and started (a boolean flag that says whether a game is in progress).</li><li>Resetting means assigning each one its initial value again: level = 0, gamePattern = [], started = false.</li><li>userClickedPattern does not need resetting here because nextSequence() already empties it at the start of every round.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why these three values restart the game (Extra)</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The keydown handler only starts a game when started is false (if (!started) {...}). While started stays true after a loss, key presses are ignored, so the game cannot restart. Setting it back to false re-arms that handler.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> level must go back to 0 because nextSequence() increments it before displaying it; starting from 0 makes the first round of the new game show 'Level 1'.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> gamePattern must be a new empty array; otherwise the new game would continue appending to the old sequence and the player would have to repeat the previous game's colours.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Putting the reset in its own named function keeps checkAnswer() readable: the else branch handles the game-over feedback (sound, red flash, title text) and then hands off to startOver().</li></ul>\n<h3>Where to call it</h3>\n<ul><li>Call startOver() inside the else branch of checkAnswer(), i.e. the branch that runs when the colour just clicked does not match gamePattern at the same index.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Call it after the game-over feedback code from Step 9, so the title change to 'Game Over, Press Any Key to Restart' is already set when the state is wiped.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">The reset function and where it is called</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function startOver() {\n  level = 0;\n  gamePattern = [];\n  started = false;\n}\n\n// inside checkAnswer(currentLevel)\n} else {\n  // ...Step 9 game-over feedback...\n  startOver();\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Simon Step 10: restart the game</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> game.js from Step 9: the game detects a wrong answer, plays wrong.mp3, flashes the body with the game-over class and changes the h1 to 'Game Over, Press Any Key to Restart', but pressing a key does nothing because the state is never reset.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> After a game over, pressing any key starts a brand-new game at Level 1 with a fresh random sequence.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Declare a new function named startOver() in game.js.</li><li>In its body, set level back to 0, set gamePattern to an empty array, and set started back to false.</li><li>Inside checkAnswer(), in the else branch that handles a wrong click, add a call to startOver() after the game-over code.</li><li>Test: play until you make a mistake, see the game-over message, press a key and confirm the title shows Level 1 and only one button flashes.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The game loops indefinitely: lose, press any key, and a new game begins from Level 1 with no leftover colours from the previous sequence.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// game.js - wrong-answer branch of checkAnswer() (from Step 9) now ends with startOver()\nfunction checkAnswer(currentLevel) {\n  if (gamePattern[currentLevel] === userClickedPattern[currentLevel]) {\n    if (userClickedPattern.length === gamePattern.length) {\n      setTimeout(function () {\n        nextSequence();\n      }, 1000);\n    }\n  } else {\n    playSound(&quot;wrong&quot;);\n    $(&quot;body&quot;).addClass(&quot;game-over&quot;);\n    setTimeout(function () {\n      $(&quot;body&quot;).removeClass(&quot;game-over&quot;);\n    }, 200);\n    $(&quot;#level-title&quot;).text(&quot;Game Over, Press Any Key to Restart&quot;);\n    startOver();\n  }\n}\n\n// Step 10: put the three state variables back to their initial values\nfunction startOver() {\n  level = 0;        // nextSequence() adds 1, so the new game shows Level 1\n  gamePattern = []; // discard the old computer sequence\n  started = false;  // lets the keydown/keypress handler start a new game\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting to reset started: the keydown listener's if (!started) guard then blocks every restart attempt.</li><li>Resetting level to 1 instead of 0, which makes the restarted game open on Level 2 because nextSequence() adds one first.</li><li>Defining startOver() but never calling it from the wrong-answer branch.</li><li>Declaring level, gamePattern or started with var inside startOver(); that creates new local variables and leaves the globals unchanged.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l182", "<p class=\"summary\">Answer page for the last Simon step. The solution download (Course Resources button, top left of the lecture screen) is the finished app, with each challenge step labelled in comments; the notes below reconstruct the complete game.js with the Step 10 startOver() logic.</p>\n<h3>How to use the answer download</h3>\n<ul><li>The download is the whole finished project, not just Step 10, so it doubles as the reference for every earlier step. Each step is marked with a comment so you can jump to the part you are checking.</li><li>It is reached from the Course Resources button in the top-left corner of the lecture player.</li><li>Your version does not have to match line for line. Different code that produces the same behaviour (same sequence logic, sounds, animations and restart) counts as a correct solution.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 10 solution: startOver()</h3>\n<ul><li>startOver() reassigns the three globals: level = 0, gamePattern = [], started = false.</li><li>It is called at the end of the else branch in checkAnswer(), after the wrong sound, the red game-over flash and the 'Game Over, Press Any Key to Restart' heading.</li><li>Because started is false again, the next key press passes the if (!started) check in the keyboard listener, which sets the heading, calls nextSequence() and flips started back to true.</li><li>nextSequence() clears userClickedPattern and increments level from 0 to 1, so the new game starts cleanly.</li></ul>\n<h3>How the finished game fits together (all ten steps)</h3>\n<ul><li>State: buttonColours = ['red','blue','green','yellow'] (lookup table for random picks), gamePattern (computer's sequence), userClickedPattern (player's clicks this round), started (game in progress?), level (round number).</li><li>Start (Step 7): $(document).keypress(...) runs once per key press; the started flag makes sure only the first press after load or after a game over starts a game.</li><li>New round (Steps 2-3): nextSequence() resets userClickedPattern, increments level and updates #level-title, picks Math.floor(Math.random() * 4) (an integer 0-3), looks up that colour, pushes it onto gamePattern, flashes the button with .fadeIn(100).fadeOut(100).fadeIn(100) and plays its sound.</li><li>Player input (Steps 4-6): $('.btn').click(...) reads the clicked div's id with $(this).attr('id'), pushes it onto userClickedPattern, plays the matching sound and calls animatePress() to add the .pressed class for 100 ms.</li><li>Checking (Step 8): checkAnswer(userClickedPattern.length - 1) passes the index of the most recent click. If gamePattern and userClickedPattern agree at that index the click was right; if the player has now matched the whole sequence (lengths equal), nextSequence() is scheduled with setTimeout(..., 1000).</li><li>Game over (Step 9): on a mismatch, play wrong.mp3, add the game-over class to body and remove it after 200 ms, and change the heading text.</li><li>Restart (Step 10): startOver() resets the state so the keyboard listener can start again.</li><li>Helpers: playSound(name) builds new Audio('sounds/' + name + '.mp3') and calls .play(); animatePress(currentColour) adds then removes the pressed class via setTimeout.</li></ul>\n<h3>Reference repo differences</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo names the script script.js and listens with $(document).keydown(...); the course project uses game.js. keydown and keypress both work here; keydown also fires for keys like Shift and arrows, and keypress is deprecated in modern browsers.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Bug in the reference nextSequence(): it calls playSound(randomNumber), which requests sounds/0.mp3 to sounds/3.mp3 (files that do not exist), so the computer's flash is silent. The call should be playSound(randomChosenColour).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference index.html has a typo, &lt;div lass=&quot;row&quot;&gt; instead of class=&quot;row&quot;, and a personal footer; both are stripped from these notes.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Complete game.js for the finished Simon game (reconstructed; reference bug fixed)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var buttonColours = [&quot;red&quot;, &quot;blue&quot;, &quot;green&quot;, &quot;yellow&quot;];\nvar gamePattern = [];\nvar userClickedPattern = [];\nvar started = false;\nvar level = 0;\n\n// Step 7: first key press starts the game\n$(document).keypress(function () {\n  if (!started) {\n    $(&quot;#level-title&quot;).text(&quot;Level &quot; + level);\n    nextSequence();\n    started = true;\n  }\n});\n\n// Steps 4-6: record, sound and animate each click, then check it\n$(&quot;.btn&quot;).click(function () {\n  var userChosenColour = $(this).attr(&quot;id&quot;);\n  userClickedPattern.push(userChosenColour);\n  playSound(userChosenColour);\n  animatePress(userChosenColour);\n  checkAnswer(userClickedPattern.length - 1);\n});\n\n// Steps 8-10\nfunction checkAnswer(currentLevel) {\n  if (gamePattern[currentLevel] === userClickedPattern[currentLevel]) {\n    if (userClickedPattern.length === gamePattern.length) {\n      setTimeout(function () {\n        nextSequence();\n      }, 1000);\n    }\n  } else {\n    playSound(&quot;wrong&quot;);\n    $(&quot;body&quot;).addClass(&quot;game-over&quot;);\n    $(&quot;#level-title&quot;).text(&quot;Game Over, Press Any Key to Restart&quot;);\n    setTimeout(function () {\n      $(&quot;body&quot;).removeClass(&quot;game-over&quot;);\n    }, 200);\n    startOver();\n  }\n}\n\n// Steps 2-3\nfunction nextSequence() {\n  userClickedPattern = [];\n  level++;\n  $(&quot;#level-title&quot;).text(&quot;Level &quot; + level);\n  var randomNumber = Math.floor(Math.random() * 4);\n  var randomChosenColour = buttonColours[randomNumber];\n  gamePattern.push(randomChosenColour);\n  $(&quot;#&quot; + randomChosenColour).fadeIn(100).fadeOut(100).fadeIn(100);\n  playSound(randomChosenColour);\n}\n\nfunction animatePress(currentColour) {\n  $(&quot;#&quot; + currentColour).addClass(&quot;pressed&quot;);\n  setTimeout(function () {\n    $(&quot;#&quot; + currentColour).removeClass(&quot;pressed&quot;);\n  }, 100);\n}\n\nfunction playSound(name) {\n  var audio = new Audio(&quot;sounds/&quot; + name + &quot;.mp3&quot;);\n  audio.play();\n}\n\n// Step 10\nfunction startOver() {\n  level = 0;\n  gamePattern = [];\n  started = false;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">index.html body for the game (reference typo and footer removed)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h1 id=&quot;level-title&quot;&gt;Press A Key to Start&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;div class=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;row&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;div type=&quot;button&quot; id=&quot;green&quot; class=&quot;btn green&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;div type=&quot;button&quot; id=&quot;red&quot; class=&quot;btn red&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;row&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;div type=&quot;button&quot; id=&quot;yellow&quot; class=&quot;btn yellow&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;div type=&quot;button&quot; id=&quot;blue&quot; class=&quot;btn blue&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;script src=&quot;https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.7.1/jquery.min.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;script src=&quot;game.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">The two CSS classes the script toggles</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.pressed {\n  box-shadow: 0 0 20px white;\n  background-color: grey;\n}\n\n.game-over {\n  background-color: red;\n  opacity: 0.8;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Simon Step 10 answer: restart the game</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Your game.js after attempting Step 10, plus the finished solution project from Course Resources.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Confirm the restart works and compare your full script with the finished version.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Download the solution from the Course Resources button and unzip it.</li><li>Find the Step 10 comment and compare startOver() with yours: three assignments, level 0, empty gamePattern, started false.</li><li>Check that startOver() is called inside the else branch of checkAnswer().</li><li>Play several games in a row to verify each restart begins at Level 1 with a single flash.</li><li>Optionally walk through the other labelled steps to tidy any earlier differences.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A complete, replayable Simon game: key press to start, growing random sequence with sounds and flashes, red flash plus game-over heading on a mistake, and a fresh game on the next key press.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// inside checkAnswer(), wrong-answer branch\n} else {\n  playSound(&quot;wrong&quot;);\n  $(&quot;body&quot;).addClass(&quot;game-over&quot;);\n  $(&quot;#level-title&quot;).text(&quot;Game Over, Press Any Key to Restart&quot;);\n  setTimeout(function () {\n    $(&quot;body&quot;).removeClass(&quot;game-over&quot;);\n  }, 200);\n  startOver();\n}\n\nfunction startOver() {\n  level = 0;        // nextSequence() bumps this to 1\n  gamePattern = []; // forget the old sequence\n  started = false;  // re-arm the key listener\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Treating a different-but-working solution as wrong; matching behaviour is what counts.</li><li>Copying the reference repo's playSound(randomNumber) line, which silences the computer's turn because no numbered mp3 files exist.</li><li>Comparing whole arrays with === (gamePattern === userClickedPattern is always false for two different arrays); compare one index at a time as checkAnswer() does.</li><li>Calling nextSequence() immediately instead of after a delay, so the next flash overlaps the player's last click.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l183", "<p class=\"summary\">Wrap-up page for the Simon Game challenge: a link to a live hosted version of the finished game and a pointer to the complete solution in Course Resources.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>A hosted copy of the finished game is at https://londonappbrewery.github.io/Simon-Game/ (a GitHub Pages site); use it to compare behaviour, timing and sounds with your own build.</li><li>The full completed project (index.html, styles.css, game.js and the sounds folder) is downloadable from the blue Course Resources button in the top-left corner of the lecture screen, with every challenge step marked in the code.</li><li>The project exercises the JavaScript and jQuery sections together: arrays, functions, conditionals, event listeners, setTimeout, jQuery selectors, class toggling, animations and the Audio object.</li><li>The complete reconstructed game.js and a list of differences in the reference repo are in the Step 10 Answer notes (lecture 182).</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Finished project layout</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">Simon Game/\n  index.html   (h1#level-title + 2x2 grid of .btn divs; loads jQuery, then game.js)\n  styles.css   (button colours, .pressed, .game-over, Press Start 2P font)\n  game.js      (all game logic)\n  sounds/      (red.mp3, blue.mp3, green.mp3, yellow.mp3, wrong.mp3)</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Opening the downloaded index.html from a zipped folder can stop the sound files loading; extract the archive first.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l184", "<p class=\"summary\">A short study tip after the Simon Game: when a bug or concept has you stuck and frustrated, step away from the screen rather than forcing it.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Staring at the same code for too long makes it harder, not easier, to spot the problem. A break lets the brain reset.</li><li>Sleeping on a problem works: review it before bed and try again the next morning, when fixes often seem obvious.</li><li>Physical activity (a walk, a run, a swim) helps clear your head before returning to the code.</li><li>Getting stuck is normal for every developer. Avoid beating yourself up; the bug will still be there when you come back fresh.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Grinding on one bug for hours without a break, which usually makes the frustration and the confusion worse.</li></ul>");
